WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.820 --> 00:00:02.550
Film Courage: Who is your character Butter?

00:00:02.550 --> 00:00:09.670
Alex Kersting, Actor: Butter is a very overweight
kid.

00:00:09.670 --> 00:00:14.360
He’s not really comfortable in his own skin.

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:15.659
He doesn’t really fit in.

00:00:15.659 --> 00:00:20.130
He’s the outcast, the misfit.

00:00:20.130 --> 00:00:25.929
Nobody even knows what his real name is even
though he’s never moved around him but don’t

00:00:25.929 --> 00:00:32.000
even call him by his real name, they call
him “Butter,” a much, much loathed nickname.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:35.789
Which the movie very well establishes why
it is loathed.

00:00:35.789 --> 00:00:44.379
Film Courage: Well we often see people like
that or we’ve been them ourselves in school

00:00:44.379 --> 00:00:47.020
for whatever reason.

00:00:47.020 --> 00:00:53.970
And sometimes you see that people like this
actually have an amazing talent or that there

00:00:53.970 --> 00:00:55.350
is something very special about them.

00:00:55.350 --> 00:00:57.629
Is there something special about the Butter?

00:00:57.629 --> 00:01:01.960
Alex: Yes, I think his musical talent is really
where it shines.

00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:09.860
He is a…I wouldn’t say prodigy but basically
that level of saxophonist but basically Butter

00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:15.360
is one of those kids who could walk into Juilliard,
pick up a saxophone, not even have a reed

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:17.759
on it and still make it sound beautiful.

00:01:17.759 --> 00:01:21.290
Film Courage: But his classmates don’t see
that in him?

00:01:21.290 --> 00:01:27.640
Alex: His classmates only see him as that
kid who has to have a specially made chair,

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:33.549
who has to have a specially made desk, who
sits by himself, who drinks to cans of Coke

00:01:33.549 --> 00:01:38.700
and eats these buffet-style lunches that he
packs for himself.

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:47.229
It’s almost ridiculous the food they’ve
had me eat for the shoot but it fits the character.

00:01:47.229 --> 00:01:54.330
Film Courage: If there was a teenager right
now that was in Butter shoes whether it was

00:01:54.330 --> 00:01:59.640
just a group of kids that doesn’t like the
person (it doesn’t even have to be where

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:05.850
they are totally different) it’s just that
they got pegged as the outcast which I know

00:02:05.850 --> 00:02:10.700
happens, what would your advice be to this
person (male or female)?

00:02:10.700 --> 00:02:18.270
Alex: You have to know that you are no alone,
whether it’s at your school, your church,

00:02:18.270 --> 00:02:22.170
whatever organization you might be a part
of it doesn’t matter.

00:02:22.170 --> 00:02:28.190
Someone also feels the same way you do, whether
you don’t feel tall enough or pretty enough,

00:02:28.190 --> 00:02:32.470
buff enough or fast enough, you’re not alone,
everyone feels that way.

00:02:32.470 --> 00:02:37.280
Everyone wants to be the best in high school.

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:43.680
The fact is, it doesn’t really matter in
high school as soon as you graduate, it gets

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:45.790
so much better.

00:02:45.790 --> 00:02:52.040
All the drama you think you’re dealing with
it goes away pretty quick as soon as the caps

00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:53.240
are on your head.

00:02:53.240 --> 00:02:56.250
But be prepared of the boredom of graduation.

00:02:56.250 --> 00:03:00.810
Film Courage: So when you graduated…you
went to high school in Nevada?

00:03:00.810 --> 00:03:04.730
Alex: I went to high school in Henderson,
Nevada (Las Vegas basically).

00:03:04.730 --> 00:03:06.100
Film Courage: Okay.

00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:08.650
Now do you still go to is it UNLV?

00:03:08.650 --> 00:03:13.040
Alex: I went there for a little bit and then
I dropped out top pursue acting a little more

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:15.440
seriously.

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:20.460
School had become a bit more of a distraction
for me so this way I had more time for auditions

00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:27.000
and make a little more money with side jobs
and focus on training, characters and stories

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:33.380
that other people react to positive or negative,
something to challenge myself.

00:03:33.380 --> 00:03:38.040
Film Courage: Did your parents…were they
okay with that (you leaving college)?

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:48.730
Alex: My dad’s attitude was always you should
at least attend at some point but he knows

00:03:48.730 --> 00:03:49.910
college isn’t for everybody.

00:03:49.910 --> 00:03:55.810
It took him several, several tries and years
to graduate.

00:03:55.810 --> 00:04:01.500
My joke is if he went to college the entire
length he went he would be a doctor and he

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:05.090
only got a bachelor’s.

00:04:05.090 --> 00:04:06.350
College isn’t for everybody.

00:04:06.350 --> 00:04:10.980
Sometimes it works out, sometimes it really
just is that time where you discover yourself

00:04:10.980 --> 00:04:15.370
but it costs a lot of money to go to college
and if you don’t have the money (like I

00:04:15.370 --> 00:04:19.120
don’t), you’ve got to make a living some
how, some other way.

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:23.820
And college is going to be there forever so
you can always talk an online class pretty

00:04:23.820 --> 00:04:27.640
quick and easy., fairly inexpensively too.

00:04:27.640 --> 00:04:29.450
So that’s my recommendation.

00:04:29.450 --> 00:04:36.180
Do what is going to save you money, do what
it’s going to take to accomplish your goals.

00:04:36.180 --> 00:04:39.760
Film Courage: How did you hear about this
character and film?

00:04:39.760 --> 00:04:45.370
Alex: Well my agent submitted me through one
of the many, many acting websites Actor’s

00:04:45.370 --> 00:04:50.120
Access I think it is who he submitted me through
but there are like three or four but I’m

00:04:50.120 --> 00:04:53.410
not entirely sure which?

00:04:53.410 --> 00:05:04.000
I drove in from Vegas not having read the
script, not having read the scene, not really

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:05.900
knowing what’s going on.

00:05:05.900 --> 00:05:11.520
The casting director Tannis, she’s amazing,
she was absolutely incredible.

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:14.640
She called me down and said this is what’s
going to happen, here’s the little secret

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:17.460
so it helps your character, make sure it improves
your performance.

00:05:17.460 --> 00:05:22.060
You’re going to read this scene, we’re
going to do it a couple times, make sure you

00:05:22.060 --> 00:05:28.650
get the best take to the director, and we’re
also going to do a short scene of you improvising

00:05:28.650 --> 00:05:35.100
a saxophone solo because that is very important
for the movie.

00:05:35.100 --> 00:05:38.360
There wasn’t even a saxophone to play.

00:05:38.360 --> 00:05:39.360
“Here’s a ladle.

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:46.440
Play it to the most complicated Charlie Parker
saxophone solo ever made.”

00:05:46.440 --> 00:05:48.000
“You ready?”

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:49.000
“No?”

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:50.340
“Okay, ready?

00:05:50.340 --> 00:05:51.340
Go.”

00:05:51.340 --> 00:05:56.980
And then some how several months later I got
the official casting.

00:05:56.980 --> 00:05:59.760
Film Courage: So for several months you did
not know?

00:05:59.760 --> 00:06:00.760
You went back to college?

00:06:00.760 --> 00:06:01.760
Alex: No.

00:06:01.760 --> 00:06:07.770
I’ve been out of college for about a year
and a half now.

00:06:07.770 --> 00:06:13.520
But came down for the audition, several months
later they’re like “Hey, can you do several

00:06:13.520 --> 00:06:14.520
additional scenes?

00:06:14.520 --> 00:06:18.220
I know you’re in Vegas, can you just do
a little video and send it to me?”

00:06:18.220 --> 00:06:23.389
So I’ve got my acting coach out there in
Vegas Paul X. Campanella who is amazing by

00:06:23.389 --> 00:06:28.860
the way if you ever need a demo reel he’ll
hook you up, he’s amazing.

00:06:28.860 --> 00:06:36.790
Shot the two additional scenes, send them
the same day and then a few months later “Okay,

00:06:36.790 --> 00:06:40.250
the director still likes you, you’re still
in the mix, we want you to come in for a screen

00:06:40.250 --> 00:06:43.389
test and we also want the director to meet
you.

00:06:43.389 --> 00:06:45.710
You can do Skype or you can do it in person.”

00:06:45.710 --> 00:06:49.450
Skype was probably better for everyone’s
schedule, you can do the Skype interview.

00:06:49.450 --> 00:06:56.500
You can discuss what Paul wants to do with
the character, how I connect to the character,

00:06:56.500 --> 00:07:01.970
where our similarities lie, and then we try
to build the character around some of the

00:07:01.970 --> 00:07:06.750
similarities to me, around Butter in the book
and what Paul’s [Paul A. Kaufman, director]

00:07:06.750 --> 00:07:11.530
goals for the character were which kind of
did a little trifecta making it something

00:07:11.530 --> 00:07:12.919
a little more three dimensional.

00:07:12.919 --> 00:07:14.889
Film Courage: Did you read the book [YA genre
written by author Erin Jade Lange]?

00:07:14.889 --> 00:07:15.889
Alex: I did.

00:07:15.889 --> 00:07:16.889
I’ve actually read it about three times.

00:07:16.889 --> 00:07:18.430
It’s a really good book.

00:07:18.430 --> 00:07:21.560
I actually have a copy of it on set with me
every day.

00:07:21.560 --> 00:07:29.330
Film Courage: So you went back to Vegas and
you’re working these jobs and you’re waiting

00:07:29.330 --> 00:07:31.680
by the phone or the computer?

00:07:31.680 --> 00:07:35.389
Alex: I’m waiting just going “Anything,
anything?”

00:07:35.389 --> 00:07:39.810
August was the screen-test with the director
and I’m like “Okay, I’m feeling pretty

00:07:39.810 --> 00:07:40.810
good.

00:07:40.810 --> 00:07:41.810
I’m feeling really good.”

00:07:41.810 --> 00:07:44.380
September comes around “I’m not feeling
too good.

00:07:44.380 --> 00:07:45.410
Nothing is happening.”

00:07:45.410 --> 00:07:48.200
I lost a little hope.

00:07:48.200 --> 00:07:50.010
I thought “They went with somebody else.

00:07:50.010 --> 00:07:51.949
I would have heard by now.”

00:07:51.949 --> 00:07:58.090
Then the week after my birthday in October
they were like “Oh, yeah…you got the part.”

00:07:58.090 --> 00:08:05.090
So I run out of the bedroom in my underwear
cheering to my parents I DID IT!

00:08:05.090 --> 00:08:06.320
I’M BUTTER!

00:08:06.320 --> 00:08:07.550
I’M BUTTER!”

00:08:07.550 --> 00:08:10.490
That call woke me up because I work graveyard
security.

00:08:10.490 --> 00:08:16.270
11 in the morning my agent called “Yeah,
you got the part.”

00:08:16.270 --> 00:08:18.130
“Okay….Oh Gosh!”

00:08:18.130 --> 00:08:22.840
Film Courage: How was it going to work that
day or that night?

00:08:22.840 --> 00:08:23.840
Alex: I was excited.

00:08:23.840 --> 00:08:28.970
I was like “Can’t say anything, can’t
say anything, can’t say anything.”

00:08:28.970 --> 00:08:30.230
It eventually came out several hours later.

00:08:30.230 --> 00:08:31.230
“I don’t need this job.

00:08:31.230 --> 00:08:32.230
I got a movie.

00:08:32.230 --> 00:08:35.039
I got the lead role in a movie.

00:08:35.039 --> 00:08:37.260
I’m feeling pretty good.”

